WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.619
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Intermediate.

2
00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:07.945
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.245 --> 00:00:13.997
Unit ten: News and Media. In this unit, you will discuss the news and report what people said.

4
00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:16.112
headline news.

5
00:00:16.812 --> 00:00:19.996
headline news means the most important stories in the news.

6
00:00:20.296 --> 00:00:22.737
The flood was the headline news on every channel.

7
00:00:23.437 --> 00:00:25.822
Your turn. Say: headline news.

8
00:00:28.522 --> 00:00:29.744
headline news.

9
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.948
source of news.

10
00:00:32.648 --> 00:00:36.331
source of news means where you get information about current events.

11
00:00:36.631 --> 00:00:38.654
My main source of news is a news app.

12
00:00:39.354 --> 00:00:41.937
Your turn. Say: source of news.

13
00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:46.021
source of news.

14
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:47.867
biased.

15
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.973
biased means supporting one side unfairly.

16
00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:54.282
Some newspapers are biased towards one political party.

17
00:00:54.982 --> 00:00:57.054
Your turn. Say: biased.

18
00:00:59.754 --> 00:01:00.604
biased.

19
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:02.908
press conference.

20
00:01:03.608 --> 00:01:07.930
press conference means a meeting where someone answers questions from journalists.

21
00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.601
The minister held a press conference this morning.

22
00:01:11.301 --> 00:01:13.895
Your turn. Say: press conference.

23
00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:17.828
press conference.

24
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:20.096
current affairs.

25
00:01:20.796 --> 00:01:23.690
current affairs means important events that are happening now.

26
00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:27.197
She watches a current affairs programme every evening.

27
00:01:27.897 --> 00:01:30.979
Your turn. Say: current affairs.

28
00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:34.889
current affairs.

29
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:36.931
viral video.

30
00:01:37.631 --> 00:01:40.780
viral video means a video that spreads very quickly online.

31
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.322
The viral video of the dancing elephant got ten million views.

32
00:01:45.022 --> 00:01:47.616
Your turn. Say: viral video.

33
00:01:50.316 --> 00:01:51.375
viral video.

34
00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:53.454
influencer.

35
00:01:54.154 --> 00:01:58.220
influencer means a person who affects what their online followers think or buy.

36
00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.495
The influencer recommended a new phone to her fans.

37
00:02:02.195 --> 00:02:05.091
Your turn. Say: influencer.

38
00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:08.920
influencer.

39
00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:10.709
clickbait.

40
00:02:11.409 --> 00:02:14.964
clickbait means a misleading headline that makes you click on a link.

41
00:02:15.264 --> 00:02:18.079
'You won't believe this!' is typical clickbait.

42
00:02:18.779 --> 00:02:21.118
Your turn. Say: clickbait.

43
00:02:23.818 --> 00:02:24.796
clickbait.

44
00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:27.760
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.966
Did you see the headline news this morning?

46
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.994
Yes. The reporter said that the new bridge would open next month.

47
00:02:36.283 --> 00:02:37.261
Where did you read it?

48
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:42.793
On social media. An influencer said it was the biggest bridge in the country.

49
00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:48.454
Be careful. Social media isn't always a reliable source of news.

50
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.552
You're right. My teacher told us that a lot of posts were clickbait.

51
00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.442
Grammar focus: Reported speech (statements).

52
00:02:57.742 --> 00:03:01.959
In reported speech, the tense usually moves one step back into the past.

53
00:03:02.259 --> 00:03:04.502
Is becomes was, and will becomes would.

54
00:03:04.802 --> 00:03:08.418
For example: The bridge is open. She said the bridge was open.

55
00:03:08.718 --> 00:03:12.865
Use say without a person (He said that ...) and tell with a person (He told me that ...).

56
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.421
Now read and listen. Spotting Fake News.

57
00:03:16.721 --> 00:03:20.880
Fake news spreads six times faster than true stories on social media.

58
00:03:22.183 --> 00:03:24.914
A study said that many readers only looked at the headline.

59
00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:29.342
Clickbait headlines are designed to make us angry or curious.

60
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.938
Experts say that we should always check the source of news.

61
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.759
Before you share a viral video, ask yourself: who made it, and why?

62
00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.493
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:42.793 --> 00:03:45.385
A misleading headline that makes you click is blank.

64
00:03:45.685 --> 00:03:49.931
A, clickbait. B, current affairs. C, a press conference.

65
00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:55.939
The answer is A: clickbait.

66
00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.485
"I am tired." → She said she blank tired.

67
00:04:00.785 --> 00:04:03.731
A, is. B, was. C, will be.

68
00:04:07.717 --> 00:04:09.542
The answer is B: was.

69
00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.563
"We will win." → They said they blank win.

70
00:04:13.863 --> 00:04:16.402
A, will. B, would. C, won.

71
00:04:20.417 --> 00:04:21.975
The answer is B: would.

72
00:04:23.483 --> 00:04:25.936
Supporting one side unfairly means blank.

73
00:04:26.236 --> 00:04:29.401
A, viral. B, biased. C, reliable.

74
00:04:33.417 --> 00:04:35.219
The answer is B: biased.

75
00:04:36.717 --> 00:04:40.702
According to the text, fake news spreads blank faster than true stories.

76
00:04:41.002 --> 00:04:45.561
A, two times. B, six times. C, ten times.

77
00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.979
The answer is B: six times.

78
00:04:53.483 --> 00:04:56.914
Now it's your turn to speak. Where do you get your news, and do you trust it?

79
00:05:02.214 --> 00:05:07.255
I usually read a news app, but I check important stories on two or three different sites.

80
00:05:07.555 --> 00:05:08.986
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:09.983 --> 00:05:14.064
Great work today! You learned eight new words and reported speech (statements).

82
00:05:14.364 --> 00:05:17.910
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

83
00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.444
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

84
00:05:20.744 --> 00:05:23.652
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

